\details{
\strong{Method}

Both configurations are centred and scaled to unit sum of squares. The
rows of \code{ord} are then permuted, and the symmetric Procrustes correlation
recomputed: the sum of the singular values of the \eqn{k \times k}
cross-product of the two. The p-value is
\eqn{(1 + \#\{r_{perm} \ge r\}) / (1 + permutations)}, where, as in
vegan, permuted statistics within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the
observed one count as ties.

The test is the same whichever of \code{ord} and \code{target} is permuted, and,
because the statistic is symmetric, whichever is rotated.

\strong{Speed and reproducibility}

Each permutation is one pass over the samples, \eqn{O(n k^2)}, and a
\eqn{k \times k} singular value decomposition; permutations run in
parallel. They come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator, so
p-values are identical from run to run and for any number of \code{cpus}, and
agree with \code{vegan::protest()}'s exactly when the same \code{permutations}
matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from vegan::protest()}
\itemize{
\item The name: \code{procrustes_test()}, not \code{protest()}, so that it sorts beside
\code{\link[=procrustes]{procrustes()}}.
\item The argument order, as for \code{\link[=procrustes]{procrustes()}}: \code{procrustes_test(ord, target)} is \code{vegan::protest(target, ord)}. The statistic and p-value
are the same either way round.
\item The default \code{axes}, as for \code{\link[=procrustes]{procrustes()}}.
